AI Clinical Trial Card: Generative AI at Work

Authors: Erik Brynjolfsson, Danielle Li, Lindsey R. Raymond

AI Model Details

Provider: OpenAI
Model Name and Version: GPT-3 (exact variant not explicitly specified)
Access Method:

Custom system integrated into the firm’s internal customer-support platform

Model accessed via OpenAI API and surfaced through a bespoke chat-style interface for agents

Fine-tuning:

Yes. GPT-3 was fine-tuned on a large corpus of historical customer–agent chat transcripts

Training data weighted toward top-performing agents’ responses, giving higher importance to high-quality exemplars

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Not fully specified in the paper

No explicit mention of temperature, system prompts, or decoding constraints

AI generated real-time suggested responses to customer messages

Performance on Standard Benchmarks:

Not reported

Paper does not situate model performance relative to NLP benchmarks (e.g., GLUE), focusing instead on field outcomes

Human Participant Profile

Data Collection Period:

Fall 2020 – Winter 2021

~5% of data from October 2020

~70% from January 2021

Sample Size:

5,172 customer support agents

Population / Expertise:

Customer service agents at a large firm

Majority located in the Philippines (≈89%), with remaining agents in the U.S. and other countries

Work characterized as routine, task-oriented service labor, often considered lower-skilled relative to professional knowledge work

Demographics:

Country-level location reported; other demographics not detailed

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Not directly measured

Study predates public release of ChatGPT → participants likely had little to no prior experience with generative language models

Training / Onboarding:

Agents received access to the AI tool as part of their workflow

No extensive AI-specific training described beyond standard system rollout

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Agents responded to customer inquiries via live text-based chat

AI system generated suggested replies that agents could adopt, edit, or ignore

Interface:

Custom-built chat interface embedded in the firm’s customer support system

AI assistance appeared alongside the customer conversation

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

AI did not act autonomously or send messages directly to customers

Final control always remained with the human agent

Experimental Conditions:

Staggered rollout of AI assistance across agents

Comparison between:

Control: no AI assistance

Treatment: AI-assisted responses

Primary Outcome Measures:

Productivity, measured using multiple operational metrics:

Resolutions per Hour (RPH): number of chats successfully resolved per hour

Average Handle Time (AHT): time taken to complete a chat

Resolution Rate (RR): share of chats successfully resolved

Net Promoter Score (NPS): customer satisfaction from post-chat surveys

Main Effects:

AI assistance increased productivity substantially:

~15% increase in Resolutions per Hour (RPH) relative to control

Effects strongest for:

Less experienced agents

Lower baseline performers

Smaller or negligible effects for highly experienced agents

Notes on Interpretation

Outcomes are tightly coupled to:

Use of a fine-tuned, domain-specific model

A real-time, embedded assistive workflow

A workforce with limited prior AI exposure

Results should not be directly generalized to settings with:

Off-the-shelf models

Expert knowledge workers

Autonomous AI systems